--TEST--
A part is refused where the program wrote it for a path no grant covers or no file answers, and where a name it carries would end a segment's own header early — `rule:http-server/an-outbound-request-carries-one-body` and `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The claim is *a bound asserted on both sides*: for each of the three things a
// part can be wrong about, the value just outside the bound and the one just
// inside it, named together. A part that refused only the first would still
// look right on its own line.
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/*", 200, {body: "ok"});
Core\IO::write("inside.txt", "granted\n");

// The door, asked where the path is written rather than where the request is
// made: a part built from a path the grant does not cover never reaches a
// socket, and the program learns that at the line it wrote.
try {
    var $outside = Core\Http\Part::file("../outside.txt");
    echo "../outside.txt: built\n";
} catch (IOError $reached) {
    echo "../outside.txt: the os answered first\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "../outside.txt: refused at the door\n";
}
// Inside the grant and absent is the other half of the same question: the door
// opens and the operating system has nothing to describe.
try {
    var $missing = Core\Http\Part::file("missing.txt");
    echo "missing.txt: built\n";
} catch (IOError $nothing) {
    echo "missing.txt: nothing there\n";
}
var $inside = Core\Http\Part::file("inside.txt");
echo "inside.txt: built\n";

// A quote in a name would close the `name="…"` a segment is identified by, so
// it is refused rather than escaped — there is no escaping that makes one safe
// (`rule:security/sink-predicate`), and the refusal names the text.
try {
    var $quoted = Core\Http\Client::post("https://api.example.com/quoted", {multipart: [
        "note" => Core\Http\Part::bytes("x", "a\"; name=\"other"),
    ]});
    echo "a quoted filename: sent\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "a quoted filename: refused\n";
}
var $plain = Core\Http\Client::post("https://api.example.com/plain", {multipart: [
    "note" => Core\Http\Part::bytes("x", "plain.txt"),
]});
echo "a plain filename: sent\n";

// The boundary is drawn per call and not derived from what is being sent: the
// same map twice is the same length under two different boundaries, which is
// what stops a field whose content a user chose from writing a second segment.
var $once = Core\Http\Client::post("https://api.example.com/twice", {multipart: ["f" => "v"]});
var $twice = Core\Http\Client::post("https://api.example.com/twice", {multipart: ["f" => "v"]});
var $sent = Core\Test::sentHttp();
array<uint> $lengths = [];
array<string> $boundaries = [];
foreach ($sent as Core\Test\SentRequest $call) {
    if ($call->url() == "https://api.example.com/twice") {
        $lengths[] = Core\Bytes::length($call->body());
        $boundaries[] = Core\Str::after($call->header("content-type") ?? "", "boundary=") ?? "";
    }
}
echo "equal lengths: ", ($lengths[0] == $lengths[1]) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
echo "equal boundaries: ", ($boundaries[0] == $boundaries[1]) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# One directory wide, which is what makes the first pair of lines differ.
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
../outside.txt: refused at the door
missing.txt: nothing there
inside.txt: built
a quoted filename: refused
a plain filename: sent
equal lengths: yes
equal boundaries: no
